  "reading": "No cell clears its bar, and the L=4 train sweep is not a KILL. Best frozen n12 Fourier ratio is 0.9372 at \u03bb=0.50 \u03bc=-0.50 L=4 (SNAP Med\u03b5 0.6746, Fourier 0.7198). The same cell is 0.6391 at n6 and 0.7762 at n9. Best n12 HVA ratio is 0.9852 at \u03bb=0.50 \u03bc=-0.50 L=4 (HVA Med\u03b5 0.6847). Quantum n12 ground states (peak < 0.5 and product gap \u2265 0.03): \u03bb=0.50 \u03bc=-0.50, \u03bb=1.00 \u03bc=-0.50, \u03bb=1.00 \u03bc=0.00. Product-gap failures, so those cells cannot PASS: \u03bb=0.50 \u03bc=0.00. A spread-out bitstring peak is not enough when a product state already reaches the ground energy. H_ZZ is pure Walsh degree 2, so the SNAP cost has one live \u03b3 per layer. Any lead over Fourier has to come from the Hamming shell. On the \u03bc=\u22120.5 cells the shell does leave the X mixer (median shell RMS off \u03c6_k=a+bk is 0.2919; median mean |\u03b2^XX| is 0.1338), and SNAP still does not clear 0.90 against Fourier or against HVA. Ratio without quantumness: \u03bb=0.50 \u03bc=0 L=2 would be a pure-family ratio (0.9615) and fails the product gap. Every seed at that best Fourier cell is above 0.90 (min 0.911). Two more seeds do not reopen a 0.90 driver PASS."
